  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and cool conditions continue through the \nweekend.\n\nCold start to the day today as radiational cooling cools the column \nresulting in temperatures in the mid to low 20s inland and low to \nmid 30s near the coast.  Should see a quick rebound by this \nafternoon with highs warming into the upper 50s to low 60s across \nthe interior. By tonight, overnight lows away from the coast likely \ndrop into the mid to low 30s. The relatively warmer low temps are \nthe result of slightly higher dew point temperatures that will range \nfrom the mid 30s to the low 40s as a weak SWrly return flow ushers \nin moisture. Warming trend continues into the early week as 925mb \ntemperatures warm significantly over the next 24 to 36 hours, up \nfrom +3C this morning to +10C by this evening. This will jump \nstart a warming trend for the week. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Above normal temperatures Tuesday through Thursday, \nwith continued dry weather.\n\nSurface high pressure continues to remain in control, essentially \nthematic of the last roughly 10 days. However aloft, the 500 mb \npattern becomes increasingly amplified as a slow moving but powerful \nfrontal system over the nation's midsection induces a plume of \nenhanced southwest flow aloft. This in turn will lead to an \namplifying 500 mb ridge and bring an anomalously warm low-level \nairmass (e.g. 925 mb temps around +12 to +14C) over Southern New \nEngland as we move into the Wednesday through Thursday timeframe. \n\nBesides a continued stretch of dry weather/no rainfall - the region \nhasn't seen an appreciable wetting rain since October 16th - the \nmain story for Tuesday through Thursday is above normal \ntemperatures. All this despite sunset times around 430 pm as we move \ntoward solar winter. The combo of continued southerly flow, the warm \nlow-level airmass and the drying soil should help boost high \ntemperatures to levels around 10 degrees above normal, especially as \nwe move into Wednesday and Thursday when the 925-850 mb thermal \nridge builds in. Expect highs to reach well into the 60s with some \nlow 70s as well (climo is around the mid 50s), and lows should also \nrun significantly above normal as well in the low to mid 50s (climo \nin the mid 30s to lower 40s).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Turning unsettled Friday into at least a part of \nnext weekend.\n\nEnsembles point to the amplified mid-level ridge from Wed/Thurs \nshifting eastward into the western Atlantic by Friday, as the slow-\nmoving frontal system over the OH/MS Valleys begins to affect \nSouthern New England. There still are quite a few uncertainties and \nunresolved details typical of a day-6 timeframe specific to this \nsystem, including rain amounts, its timing and if the system can \ndraw on a plume of subtropical moisture to enhance rain rates. \nEnsemble 24-hr QPF probabilities from Friday through Saturday show \nmoderate (40-60%) probabilities 0.5\" or more of rain with low to \nmoderate (up to 30%) probabilities of an inch or more of rain. While \ntemps should still run above normal and also run a touch humid (by \nearly Nov standards), this looks to be the next really good chance \nat much-needed wetting rainfall.",
